---
/**
 * MullastCaseStudy — "Mullast oled sa tulnud, mullaks pead sa saama."
 * Architecture mirrors OleValmisCaseStudy.astro 1:1.
 * Design tokens: #111111 / #0052cc / #f9f9f9 / #eeeeee (biophilic palette)
 */
import './MullastCaseStudy.css';
---

<div class="ml">

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       QUICK OVERVIEW — two-column grid
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="ml-overview" aria-label="Project quick overview">
    <div class="ml-overview__col">
      <div class="ml-overview__item">
        <span class="ml-overview__key">Context</span>
        <p class="ml-overview__val">Artistic research, electronic material experimentation, and interactive spatial design sprint executed at EKA.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ml-overview__item">
        <span class="ml-overview__key">The Team</span>
        <p class="ml-overview__val">Kairiin Koddala — Solo Initiative / Independent Artistic Production</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ml-overview__col">
      <div class="ml-overview__item">
        <span class="ml-overview__key">Timeframe</span>
        <p class="ml-overview__val">Conceptualization, technical prototyping, and final exhibition installation — 2025</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ml-overview__item">
        <span class="ml-overview__key">My Role</span>
        <p class="ml-overview__val">Artistic Researcher, Interaction Designer, Creative Hardware Technologist &amp; Spatial Builder</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACCORDION STAGES
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="ml-accordion" role="list">

    <!-- ── STAGE 1 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">01</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Executive Summary &amp; Strategic Intent</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Project Scope &amp; Core Vision</h3>
          <p>This project is an artistic research and interactive spatial installation that investigates the invisible electrical life present inside living flora. Through the translation of bio-electric signals — moisture levels, tension gradients, and conductivity patterns — into minimalist sound, the work creates a meditative interface between human visitors and the living plant world.</p>
          <p>The installation invites participants to physically touch living plants and soil, triggering real-time audio responses generated by the plant's own electrical activity. The result is a deeply intimate, sensory encounter that dissolves the boundary between observer and organism.</p>

          <h3 class="ml-subheading">The Cultural Framework</h3>
          <p>The title draws from an Estonian proverb rooted in the Lutheran and folk tradition: <em>"Mullast oled sa tulnud, mullaks pead sa saama."</em> — From soil you have come, to soil you shall return. This phrase encapsulates the cyclical nature of organic life, human mortality, and the deep spiritual connection between people and the earth that Estonian culture has historically maintained.</p>
          <p>The installation uses this proverb not as a morbid statement but as a grounding philosophical anchor — a reminder that the electrical signals pulsing through plant tissue are not fundamentally different from those animating human bodies. We are, at the most elemental level, the same material.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Image placeholder: Atmospheric mood collage -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">Atmospheric visual collage or early inspirational mood images</span>
            <span class="ml-media__file">Artistic research | 03</span>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Early-stage artistic research — inspirational mood board and conceptual reference imagery</figcaption>
        </figure>

      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- ── STAGE 2 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">02</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Team Alignment &amp; Agile Operational Methodology</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Process Mapping via Research Tracks</h3>
          <p>As a solo initiative, the project was structured across three parallel research and production tracks that ran concurrently throughout the development sprint:</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-track-grid">
          <div class="ml-track-card">
            <div class="ml-track-card__badge">Track 1</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Artistic Research Stream</h3>
            <p>Curated feed auditing of international bio-electric art, sound installation precedents, and Estonian folk philosophy. Mapping the conceptual territory between nature, mortality, and interactive media.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-track-card">
            <div class="ml-track-card__badge">Track 2</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Material &amp; Technical Stream</h3>
            <p>Live hardware prototyping with electrode probes, analog-to-digital converters, and signal processing logic. Testing plant species, soil compositions, and moisture variables for signal stability and sonic expressiveness.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-track-card">
            <div class="ml-track-card__badge">Track 3</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Spatial Synthesis &amp; Execution</h3>
            <p>Designing and constructing the sealed greenhouse enclosure, sourcing raw timber and translucent diffuser materials, and choreographing the visitor journey from approach to immersive contact.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Agile Operational Infrastructure</h3>
          <p>The solo agile workflow was managed through FigJam spatial mapping boards, which served as the primary project canvas for tracking research threads, hardware iteration logs, and spatial construction dimensions. ChatGPT was used as a real-time dimension tracking and calculation assistant — particularly for structural load estimates, material quantities, and signal processing parameter tuning.</p>
          <p>Sprint cycles were kept short and iterative, with each hardware prototype informing the next design decision. This tight feedback loop between material testing and conceptual development was essential to the project's evolution.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Image placeholder: Process roadmap -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">Visual representation of the project roadmap, process flow chart, or interactive canvas boards</span>
            <span class="ml-media__file">FigJam process canvas — project roadmap</span>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Solo agile process map — FigJam research canvas and sprint tracking board</figcaption>
        </figure>

      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- ── STAGE 3 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">03</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Research Methodology &amp; Data Triangulation</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Research Streams</h3>
          <p>The research methodology was built around four distinct investigative approaches, each contributing a different layer of evidence to the final installation design:</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-method-grid">
          <div class="ml-method-card">
            <div class="ml-method-card__badge">Method 1</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Self-Interview Mapping</h3>
            <p>A structured introspective process in which the artist conducted formal self-interviews to surface personal motivations, cultural memories, and emotional associations with soil, plants, and mortality. This method grounded the conceptual framework in lived experience rather than purely theoretical abstraction.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-method-card">
            <div class="ml-method-card__badge">Method 2</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Curated Feed Sifting</h3>
            <p>Systematic auditing of international artistic references through curated social media feeds, exhibition archives, and academic databases. This process — sometimes described as productive doomscrolling — surfaced key precedents in bio-electric art, plant-based sound installations, and biophilic spatial design.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-method-card">
            <div class="ml-method-card__badge">Method 3</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Artistic Reference Auditing</h3>
            <p>Deep-dive analysis of specific artists and works operating at the intersection of biology, electronics, and sound — including bio-electric music pioneers, plant-based interactive installations, and minimalist sound composers working with natural systems. Each reference was evaluated for conceptual alignment and technical transferability.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-method-card">
            <div class="ml-method-card__badge">Method 4</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Bio-Electric Sound Exploration</h3>
            <p>Hands-on laboratory-style experimentation with electrode probes inserted into living plant tissue and soil. Testing different plant species, moisture levels, and electrode placements to understand the range and character of bio-electric signals available as raw sonic material.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Research Limitations &amp; Design Constraints</h3>
          <ul class="ml-list">
            <li><strong>Signal Instability:</strong> Raw bio-electric signals from plant tissue are inherently noisy and variable. Significant smoothing and filtering logic was required to produce musically coherent output without losing the organic character of the source signal.</li>
            <li><strong>Budget Constraints:</strong> The project operated under strict material and equipment budget caps, ruling out expensive commercial bio-electric interfaces and MIDI hardware. This constraint ultimately drove the pivot toward open-source, browser-based audio solutions.</li>
            <li><strong>Species Variability:</strong> Different plant species produce significantly different signal profiles. Extensive testing was required to identify species with stable, expressive, and aesthetically appropriate signal characteristics for the installation context.</li>
            <li><strong>Spatial Enclosure Acoustics:</strong> The sealed greenhouse container created unexpected acoustic resonance patterns that required iterative spatial adjustment to achieve the intended meditative sound environment.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Image placeholder: Curated feed compilation -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">Curated feed compilation showing interactive audio-visual art references</span>
            <span class="ml-media__file">Artistic research | 03</span>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Artistic reference audit — curated compilation of bio-electric and biophilic installation precedents</figcaption>
        </figure>

      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- ── STAGE 4 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">04</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Data Analysis &amp; Synthesis — The Technical Pivot</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <p>The initial technical hypothesis centred on integrating the bio-electric signal chain with an external MIDI desktop interface — routing plant signals through a commercial hardware converter into a professional digital audio workstation. Live hardware trials rapidly disproved this approach.</p>
          <p>The external MIDI desktop integration introduced prohibitive latency, required expensive proprietary hardware, and created a visual and conceptual disconnect between the living plant material and the synthetic technology stack. The setup felt clinical, tech-centered, and antithetical to the biophilic intent of the work.</p>
          <p>This critical finding forced a <strong>Suunamuutus</strong> — a directional shift — toward a fully browser-based architecture using the Web Audio API. This pivot proved transformative: it eliminated hardware costs, reduced latency to near-zero, and allowed the entire sonic engine to be embedded invisibly within the installation structure itself.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Comparison matrix -->
        <div class="ml-comparison">
          <div class="ml-comparison__header">
            <span class="ml-comparison__title">Apparatus Comparison Matrix</span>
            <span class="ml-comparison__sub">Hardware MIDI Integration vs. Browser-Based Web Audio Architecture</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-comparison__grid">
            <div class="ml-comparison__card ml-comparison__card--closed">
              <div class="ml-comparison__card-label">Closed Synthetic Apparatus</div>
              <ul class="ml-comparison__list">
                <li>Expensive pre-made black box hardware</li>
                <li>Rigid pre-mapped musical behavior</li>
                <li>Distant, visually dominant tech setup</li>
                <li>Clinical observation mode</li>
                <li>Technology-centered focus</li>
                <li>High asset and licensing costs</li>
              </ul>
              <div class="ml-comparison__verdict ml-comparison__verdict--closed">User Impact: Clinical, tech-centered, high cost</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-comparison__divider">→</div>
            <div class="ml-comparison__card ml-comparison__card--open">
              <div class="ml-comparison__card-label">Open Biophilic Interface</div>
              <ul class="ml-comparison__list">
                <li>Affordable DIY converter arrays</li>
                <li>Pure HTML / Web Audio API logic</li>
                <li>Material interface: soil, moss, plant tissue</li>
                <li>Meditative, immersive encounter</li>
                <li>Plant-centered focus</li>
                <li>Zero-waste frame construction</li>
              </ul>
              <div class="ml-comparison__verdict ml-comparison__verdict--open">User Impact: Meditative calm, plant-centered, zero-waste</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Core Material Realities Uncovered</h3>
          <ul class="ml-list">
            <li><strong>The Necessity of Micro-Signal Smoothing:</strong> Raw bio-electric readings from plant tissue contain significant high-frequency noise. A mathematical smoothing layer — applying rolling average calculations across signal windows — was essential to produce stable, musically coherent tonal output while preserving the organic variability of the source signal.</li>
            <li><strong>The Estonian Spiritual Connection to Nature:</strong> Research into Estonian folk tradition and the Lutheran cultural framework revealed a deeply embedded philosophical relationship between people and soil that gave the project its conceptual spine. The proverb is not metaphor — it is material fact expressed as cultural memory.</li>
            <li><strong>The Impact of Spatial Enclosure:</strong> Enclosing the installation within a sealed greenhouse structure fundamentally changed the visitor's psychological state before any sonic interaction occurred. The act of entering a contained, plant-filled space triggered a measurable shift in attention and bodily awareness.</li>
            <li><strong>The Tintinnabuli Aesthetic Match:</strong> The minimalist compositional system developed by Estonian composer Arvo Pärt — Tintinnabuli, meaning "little bells" — proved to be the ideal sonic framework for the installation. Its sparse, resonant, and meditative character aligned precisely with the bio-electric signal's natural rhythm and the installation's contemplative intent.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- ── STAGE 5 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">05</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Systems &amp; Experience Mapping</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Macro-Systemic Conceptual Diagram</h3>
          <p>The technical signal chain maps a direct path from living biological material through precision analog-to-digital conversion into a browser-based audio synthesis engine — with no proprietary hardware intermediaries in the chain.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- System flow diagram -->
        <div class="ml-arch">
          <div class="ml-arch__label">Signal Flow Architecture</div>
          <div class="ml-arch__diagram">
            <div class="ml-arch__node ml-arch__node--input">
              <div class="ml-arch__node-title">Living Plant Tissue</div>
              <div class="ml-arch__node-sub">Bio-electric signals + Human touch stimuli</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-arch__arrow">──►</div>
            <div class="ml-arch__node ml-arch__node--process">
              <div class="ml-arch__node-title">ADS1115 High-Accuracy Converter</div>
              <div class="ml-arch__node-sub">16-bit ADC · Micro-signal smoothing layer · Rolling average filter</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-arch__arrow">──►</div>
            <div class="ml-arch__node ml-arch__node--output">
              <div class="ml-arch__node-title">Web Audio API Sound Engine</div>
              <div class="ml-arch__node-sub">Minimalist browser-based synthesis · Tintinnabuli tonal mapping</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Micro-Experience Map: The Inside Journey</h3>
          <p>The visitor experience was choreographed as a four-stage sensory progression, each phase designed to deepen the participant's connection to the living material and the sonic environment:</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Horizontal journey map -->
        <div class="ml-journey">
          <div class="ml-journey__track">
            <div class="ml-journey__step">
              <div class="ml-journey__step-num">1</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-title">Approach &amp; Enclose</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-desc">The visitor approaches the sealed greenhouse structure. The act of physically entering the enclosure — ducking through a low threshold, surrounded by living plants — triggers an immediate shift in sensory attention. The outside world recedes.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-journey__connector">──►</div>
            <div class="ml-journey__step">
              <div class="ml-journey__step-num">2</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-title">Ground &amp; Sit</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-desc">The visitor is invited to sit or kneel on the soil floor of the enclosure. This grounding posture — physically lowering the body toward the earth — is both a practical positioning for interaction and a symbolic act of humility and presence.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-journey__connector">──►</div>
            <div class="ml-journey__step">
              <div class="ml-journey__step-num">3</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-title">Biophilic Touch</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-desc">The visitor places their hands on the living plants or directly into the soil. This contact completes the bio-electric circuit, and the installation responds with tonal sound generated from the plant's own electrical activity. The visitor becomes part of the signal chain.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ml-journey__connector">──►</div>
            <div class="ml-journey__step">
              <div class="ml-journey__step-num">4</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-title">Reflect &amp; Listen</div>
              <div class="ml-journey__step-desc">As the visitor remains still, the sound evolves with the plant's natural electrical fluctuations. The experience becomes meditative — a listening practice in which the boundary between self and organism dissolves. The proverb becomes embodied knowledge.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Core HMW prompt -->
        <blockquote class="ml-callout">
          <p class="ml-callout__quote">How might we create a spatial encounter that makes the invisible electrical life of plants audible — and in doing so, remind visitors of their own material kinship with the living world?</p>
        </blockquote>

      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- ── STAGE 6 ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <details class="ml-stage" role="listitem">
      <summary class="ml-stage__summary">
        <span class="ml-stage__num">06</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__title">Technical Infrastructure &amp; Interface Architecture</span>
        <span class="ml-stage__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ml-stage__body">

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Frontend Audio Architecture</h3>
          <p>The technical system operates across three functional layers, each handling a distinct stage of the signal-to-sound pipeline:</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-tech-grid">
          <div class="ml-tech-card">
            <div class="ml-tech-card__num">Layer 1</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Onboarding Filter Console</h3>
            <p>A lightweight browser-based interface that initialises the serial connection between the ADS1115 converter and the Web Audio engine. Handles device detection, signal calibration, and threshold configuration before the installation enters its active listening state.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-tech-card ml-tech-card--accent">
            <div class="ml-tech-card__num">Layer 2</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Mathematical Smoothing Layer</h3>
            <p>A rolling average algorithm applied to the raw ADC readings, calculating a windowed mean across the most recent signal samples. This layer eliminates high-frequency electrical noise while preserving the slow, organic fluctuations that carry musical meaning. The window size was tuned empirically through live plant testing sessions.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ml-tech-card">
            <div class="ml-tech-card__num">Layer 3</div>
            <h3 class="ml-subheading">Web Audio API Engine</h3>
            <p>The smoothed signal values are mapped to tonal parameters within the Web Audio API — primarily oscillator frequency and gain envelope — using a Tintinnabuli-inspired mapping that produces sparse, bell-like tones. The engine runs entirely in the browser with no external dependencies, ensuring zero-latency response to touch input.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ml-prose">
          <h3 class="ml-subheading">Platform Ecosystem: Sustainability Framework</h3>
          <p>The installation's physical construction was designed around a zero-waste material philosophy, sourcing all structural components from circular material partnerships and reclaimed sources:</p>
          <ul class="ml-list">
            <li><strong>Raw Timber Beams:</strong> Unfinished structural timber sourced from local suppliers, left untreated to maintain material honesty and allow natural weathering within the gallery context.</li>
            <li><strong>Circular Material Partnerships:</strong> Soil, moss, and plant material sourced through partnerships with local nurseries and urban composting initiatives, ensuring all organic material could be returned to productive use after the exhibition.</li>
            <li><strong>Translucent Diffuser Canvas:</strong> A lightweight translucent fabric stretched across the timber frame to create the greenhouse enclosure effect — diffusing ambient gallery light into a soft, even interior glow while maintaining visual permeability from outside.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Image placeholder 1: Hardware schematic -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">Technical Hardware Schematic Sheet showing Electrode Probes, ADS1115 Converter, and Serial Connection Paths</span>
            <span class="ml-media__file">Material/Tech research | 05</span>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Hardware schematic — electrode probe array, ADS1115 converter wiring, and serial connection architecture</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Image placeholder 2: Spatial construction sketches -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">Spatial Construction Sketches and Atmospheric Prototype Layouts for the Sealed Greenhouse Container</span>
            <span class="ml-media__file">Final outcome | 07</span>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Spatial construction sketches — sealed greenhouse enclosure dimensions and atmospheric prototype layouts</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Image placeholder 3: Gallery documentation -->
        <figure class="ml-media">
          <div class="ml-media__placeholder">
            <span class="ml-media__icon">⬜</span>
            <span class="ml-media__text">High-Fidelity Gallery Documentation Photographs — Completed Exhibition Installation Views</span>
